RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] 
##########################################################################
# BOUNDED BUFFER LIVELOCK DEMO  (model of the full-buffer deadlock)
#
#   q    = pending queue, oldest request first. A request leaves ONLY when it completes.
#   buf  = bounded FIFO of request ids, capacity 4.
#   port = ONE service per cycle (service port). A service pops buf.front().
#
# Start state: requests 4..7 already fill buf, the oldest requests
# 0..3 are still waiting in q. Demand is constant: whenever a request completes, a new
# younger one enters q.
#
# Each cycle every request in q is visited oldest-first, in order:
#   not in buf -> try to insert; if buf is full the insert fails, but the request may
#                 still burn the port to pop the front ('queue-only service');
#   in buf     -> try the port; it completes only if buf.front() is ITS OWN entry.
##########################################################################
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] 
==========================================================================
 VARIANT: BUGGY (result dropped on queue-only service)
==========================================================================
  Behaviour : the queue-only service pops the front, but the result is thrown away.
  Why bad   : the owner of the popped entry is not marked done and is not in buf, so it must
              be inserted and serviced AGAIN - and the port is burned by the oldest request every
              cycle before the owner gets its turn.
  Expect    : LIVELOCK - everyone keeps trying, the port is busy every cycle, 0 completions.
  narrating the first 6 cycles:
RPT_WARNING @ 50 [BUF] LIVELOCK suspected: no request completed for 50 consecutive cycles (buf occupancy 4/4)
RPT_ERROR @ 200 [BUF] oldest request (id 0) never completed in 200 cycles -> oldest request blocked -> DEADLOCK  [BUGGY (result dropped on queue-only service)]
RPT_INFO @ 200 [DEMO] RESULT after 200 cycles:
    completed requests        : 0
    oldest request (id 0) : NEVER completed -> in-order completion stops -> DEADLOCK
    peak buffer occupancy : 4/4
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] 
==========================================================================
 VARIANT: RESULT_KEPT (no reserved slot)
==========================================================================
  Behaviour : the queue-only service pops the front AND remembers the result: the owner is
              marked ready and completes on its next visit without needing the port.
  Why better: no work is wasted, so the system moves.
  Remaining : right after the oldest request's failed insert, younger requests refill the freed slot
              (they are visited later in the same pass). The oldest request never finds a free
              slot -> HEAD STARVATION. With in-order completion the whole system stalls again.
  Expect    : many completions, but request 0 NEVER completes.
  narrating the first 6 cycles:
RPT_ERROR @ 200 [BUF] oldest request (id 0) never completed in 200 cycles -> oldest request blocked -> DEADLOCK  [RESULT_KEPT (no reserved slot)]
RPT_INFO @ 200 [DEMO] RESULT after 200 cycles:
    completed requests        : 200
    oldest request (id 0) : NEVER completed -> in-order completion stops -> DEADLOCK
    peak buffer occupancy : 4/4
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] 
==========================================================================
 VARIANT: RESERVED_SLOT (fix)
==========================================================================
  Behaviour : result kept (as above) PLUS one slot is reserved for the oldest request. Every other
              request may fill the buffer only up to capacity-1.
  Why it works: the oldest request always has a free slot to enter, so it can never be starved by
              younger requests; once it is in buf it is serviced in FIFO order.
  Expect    : request 0 completes within a few cycles, then ~1 completion per cycle (1 port).
  narrating the first 6 cycles:
RPT_INFO @ 200 [DEMO] RESULT after 200 cycles:
    completed requests        : 200
    oldest request (id 0) : completed at cycle 5
    peak buffer occupancy : 4/4
RPT_INFO @ 200 [DEMO] 
TAKEAWAY
  LIVELOCK  : everybody is busy, nothing completes (work is done and thrown away).
  STARVATION: the resource works, others progress, but the oldest requester never gets in.
  FIX       : never waste a service (keep the result) AND guarantee the oldest requester
              a slot (reserve one), so progress is guaranteed from the head of the queue.

